// Domain 2: Reliability and Business Continuity (22%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d2-01", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs a web application on Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group. Load changes throughout the day without a fixed pattern. The company must keep average CPU utilization near 50%.\n\nWhich scaling policy will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["A target tracking policy on ASGAverageCPUUtilization with a target of 50",
      "A step scaling policy with separate alarms at 40%, 50%, and 60% CPU",
      "A scheduled action that changes the desired capacity every hour",
      "A simple scaling policy that adds one instance when CPU passes 50%"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Target tracking works like a thermostat. You choose a metric and a target value, and EC2 Auto Scaling creates and manages the CloudWatch alarms and adjusts capacity in both directions to keep the metric near the target.",
      "Step scaling can work, but you must create and tune the alarms and step adjustments yourself, which is more effort.",
      "Scheduled actions suit predictable patterns, and this load has no fixed pattern.",
      "Simple scaling adds one instance at a time and waits for a cooldown before it acts again, so it reacts slowly to large changes. It also needs a separate scale-in policy."] },

{ id: "d2-02", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs a stateless API on Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer. Load tests show that each instance handles up to 1,000 requests per minute before latency rises. CPU utilization stays below 40% even at that point.\n\nWhich scaling configuration will keep latency low?",
  o: ["A target tracking policy on ALBRequestCountPerTarget with a target of about 800",
      "A target tracking policy on ASGAverageCPUUtilization with a target of 40",
      "A target tracking policy on ASGAverageNetworkIn with a target of 1,000",
      "A step scaling policy that adds instances when HealthyHostCount drops"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The instances slow down because of request volume, not CPU. Request count per target measures that load directly, and a target below the 1,000 limit leaves headroom while new instances start.",
      "CPU stays below 40% even when latency rises, so a CPU target would not add instances in time.",
      "Network bytes in are not the same as requests, and 1,000 bytes is not a meaningful target for this API.",
      "HealthyHostCount drops when targets fail health checks. It does not track the request load that causes the latency."] },

{ id: "d2-03", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an internal HR application that runs in an Auto Scaling group. The application has almost no traffic at night. Every weekday at 08:00, 4,000 employees sign in within 10 minutes, and the group's target tracking policy cannot add instances fast enough.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to handle the morning peak?",
  o: ["Add a scheduled action that raises the minimum capacity at 07:45 each weekday and lowers it in the evening.",
      "Lower the target value of the target tracking policy to run twice the instances that the group needs.",
      "Change the default instance warmup to count new instances toward the group's metrics sooner.",
      "Replace the target tracking policy with a simple scaling policy that has a shorter cooldown period."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The peak is predictable, so a scheduled action can add capacity before users arrive. Target tracking stays in place to handle changes during the day, and a second scheduled action lowers the minimum in the evening to save cost.",
      "Running twice the needed capacity all day and night wastes money, especially at night when there is almost no traffic.",
      "Warmup controls when a new instance's metrics are counted. It does not start instances any earlier, so they would still be late.",
      "A simple scaling policy still reacts only after the load arrives, so instances would still be late for the peak."] },

{ id: "d2-04", d: 2,
  q: "A company's retail website runs on Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group. Traffic follows a repeating daily and weekly pattern. New instances take 8 minutes to boot and warm their caches. Capacity must be ready before traffic increases, without a manually maintained schedule.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add a predictive scaling policy. Keep a target tracking policy for unexpected changes.",
      "Create scheduled actions for each hour of the week. Update them every month.",
      "Use step scaling with larger step adjustments to start more instances at once.",
      "Enable detailed monitoring to evaluate the scaling alarms every minute."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Predictive scaling uses machine learning on the group's history to forecast load and launches capacity ahead of the forecast, which covers the 8-minute boot time. Target tracking still reacts to changes that the forecast does not predict.",
      "A full set of scheduled actions must be built and kept up to date by hand, which the company wants to avoid.",
      "Step scaling still reacts after the load arrives, so new instances would still be 8 minutes late.",
      "More frequent metrics help scaling react slightly sooner, but capacity still arrives after the traffic does."] },

{ id: "d2-05", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Auto Scaling group of Amazon EC2 instances that run an initialization script that takes 12 minutes. During sudden spikes, users see errors until the new instances finish initializing. The company needs faster scale-out without paying for running instances that do not serve traffic.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add a warm pool of pre-initialized instances in the Stopped state.",
      "Raise the minimum capacity of the group by the size of a typical spike.",
      "Add a launch lifecycle hook that holds new instances in Pending:Wait.",
      "Lower the health check grace period."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A warm pool keeps instances that have already run their initialization. Stopped instances incur no instance charges, only EBS storage, and they move into service much faster than new instances that start from scratch.",
      "A higher minimum capacity keeps extra instances running without serving traffic, which is the cost the company wants to avoid.",
      "A lifecycle hook gives an instance time to finish setup before it enters service. It does not shorten the 12-minute setup.",
      "A shorter grace period does not make initialization faster. Instances could be marked unhealthy and replaced before they are ready."] },

{ id: "d2-06", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Auto Scaling group of Amazon EC2 instances that write application logs to local disk. When the group scales in, the logs on terminated instances are lost. The logs must be uploaded to Amazon S3 before each instance is terminated.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add a termination lifecycle hook that runs a script to upload the logs before the instance terminates.",
      "Enable scale-in protection for all instances to prevent the group from terminating them.",
      "Set the termination policy to OldestLaunchTemplate to keep the newer instances.",
      "Increase the deregistration delay of the target group to give the logs time to upload."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A termination lifecycle hook holds the instance in the Terminating:Wait state. An EventBridge rule can run a script on the instance, for example with Systems Manager Run Command, that uploads the logs and then completes the lifecycle action so termination continues.",
      "Scale-in protection stops the group from removing instances, which breaks scale-in and increases cost.",
      "A termination policy decides which instance is removed. The removed instance still loses its logs.",
      "Deregistration delay lets in-flight requests finish at the load balancer. It does not run scripts or upload files."] },

{ id: "d2-07", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Auto Scaling group of Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB). The instances sometimes have a hung web server process. The ALB marks those targets as unhealthy and stops sending them traffic, but the Auto Scaling group never replaces them. The EC2 status checks pass.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do so that the group replaces these instances?",
  o: ["Enable Elastic Load Balancing health checks for the Auto Scaling group.",
      "Configure detailed monitoring for every instance in the Auto Scaling group.",
      "Lower the ALB health check interval from 30 seconds to 5 seconds.",
      "Add an Amazon CloudWatch alarm that reboots instances on StatusCheckFailed."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["By default, an Auto Scaling group uses only EC2 status checks, which pass because the instance is still running. When ELB health checks are enabled, the group also treats targets that fail the load balancer health check as unhealthy and replaces them.",
      "Detailed monitoring adds 1-minute metrics. It does not change how the group decides that an instance is unhealthy.",
      "A shorter interval detects the failure sooner at the load balancer, but the group still ignores the load balancer's health status.",
      "The status checks pass, so this alarm would never fire for a hung web server process."] },

{ id: "d2-08", d: 2,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer enables Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) health checks for an Auto Scaling group. New instances are now terminated and replaced repeatedly. Each instance needs about 6 minutes to install dependencies before the application responds. The health check grace period is 60 seconds.\n\nWhat should the CloudOps engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Increase the health check grace period to longer than the startup time.",
      "Switch the Auto Scaling group back to Amazon EC2 status checks only.",
      "Lower the unhealthy threshold count on the target group to 2.",
      "Enable scale-in protection for each instance as it launches."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The grace period tells the group to ignore health check results for a new instance until it has had time to start. A value longer than the 6-minute startup stops the group from replacing instances that are still initializing.",
      "This stops the loop, but the group could no longer replace instances whose application stops responding.",
      "A lower threshold marks targets unhealthy even faster, which makes the problem worse.",
      "Scale-in protection affects scale-in decisions. It does not stop the group from replacing instances that it considers unhealthy."] },

{ id: "d2-09", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs a batch processing fleet in an Auto Scaling group. The workload can tolerate interruptions. The company must minimize cost, keep at least 2 On-Demand Instances at all times, and reduce the chance of Spot Instance interruptions.\n\nWhich configuration will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["A mixed instances policy with an On-Demand base capacity of 2, several instance types, and the price-capacity-optimized Spot strategy",
      "A mixed instances policy with an On-Demand base capacity of 2, a single instance type, and the lowest-price Spot strategy",
      "A launch template for Spot Instances of one instance type, and a scheduled action that adds 2 On-Demand Instances daily",
      "Two Auto Scaling groups: one with 2 On-Demand Instances, and one with Spot Instances that use a single instance type"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The On-Demand base capacity keeps 2 On-Demand Instances, and the rest of the group can use Spot. Allowing several instance types gives Auto Scaling more Spot capacity pools, and price-capacity-optimized chooses pools that have low prices and a low chance of interruption.",
      "One instance type limits Spot to fewer pools, and lowest-price ignores available capacity, which raises the risk of interruptions.",
      "A scheduled action does not guarantee that 2 On-Demand Instances are always running, and one Spot instance type increases interruption risk.",
      "Two groups add management effort, and a single Spot instance type still increases interruption risk."] },

{ id: "d2-10", d: 2,
  q: "A company has worker instances in an Auto Scaling group that process video jobs that can run for up to 45 minutes. When the group scales in, it sometimes terminates an instance in the middle of a job.\n\nHow can a CloudOps engineer prevent the group from terminating instances that are processing jobs?",
  o: ["Have the worker enable instance scale-in protection when a job starts. Disable it when the job ends.",
      "Change the termination policy to terminate the instance with the newest launch template.",
      "Increase the cooldown period of the scaling policy to 45 minutes to scale in less often.",
      "Set the minimum capacity equal to the maximum capacity to stop the group from scaling in."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Instance scale-in protection stops Auto Scaling from choosing that instance during scale-in. Setting it through the API when a job starts and clearing it when the job finishes protects only busy instances, while idle instances can still be removed.",
      "The termination policy only changes which instance is chosen. A busy instance can still be terminated.",
      "A longer cooldown delays scaling activities but does not protect instances that are in the middle of a job.",
      "A fixed size removes elasticity and keeps idle instances running, which raises cost."] },

{ id: "d2-11", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Auto Scaling group of worker instances that process messages from an Amazon SQS queue. Each instance processes about 100 messages per minute. The number of instances must grow and shrink so that any backlog is cleared within about 5 minutes.\n\nWhich scaling approach will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Publish a backlog per instance metric. Use target tracking with a target of 500 messages per instance.",
      "Use target tracking on the group's average CPU utilization with a target value of 70%.",
      "Use step scaling on the queue's NumberOfMessagesSent metric with one step for each 100 messages.",
      "Use a scheduled action that sets the desired capacity to the queue length at the top of each hour."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Backlog per instance is ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible divided by the number of running instances. Each instance clears 100 messages per minute, so 500 messages per instance is about 5 minutes of work. Target tracking on this metric adds and removes instances as the queue grows and shrinks.",
      "CPU does not show how many messages are waiting. Workers can run at low CPU while the backlog grows.",
      "Messages sent shows the arrival rate, not the backlog, and it ignores how many instances are already running.",
      "An hourly schedule cannot react to a backlog that changes within minutes."] },

{ id: "d2-12", d: 2,
  q: "A company has a new mobile game that uses an Amazon DynamoDB table in provisioned capacity mode with auto scaling enabled. Traffic is unpredictable and sometimes doubles within seconds after a social media post. Requests are throttled during these jumps.\n\nWhich solution will MOST effectively stop the throttling?",
  o: ["Switch the table to on-demand capacity mode.",
      "Lower the auto scaling target utilization to 50%.",
      "Add a global secondary index to the table.",
      "Enable DynamoDB Streams for the table."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["On-demand mode serves traffic without capacity planning and instantly accommodates up to double the table's previous peak. Auto scaling in provisioned mode adjusts over minutes, so it cannot keep up with spikes that arrive within seconds.",
      "A lower target adds more headroom, but auto scaling still reacts after the spike, so sudden jumps are still throttled.",
      "A global secondary index has its own capacity needs. It does not add capacity to the base table.",
      "Streams capture item changes for other consumers. They do not add read or write capacity."] },

{ id: "d2-13", d: 2,
  q: "A company has a product catalog in an Amazon DynamoDB table. Reads outnumber writes by 50 to 1, and the same items are read repeatedly. The application needs microsecond read latency, and the company wants the fewest possible code changes.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add a DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) cluster. Point the DynamoDB client at the DAX endpoint.",
      "Add an Amazon ElastiCache cluster, and write cache logic for every read and write in the application.",
      "Create a global table in a second Region. Send half of the reads to the replica table.",
      "Switch the table to on-demand capacity mode."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["DAX is an in-memory cache built for DynamoDB that delivers microsecond read latency for cached items. It is compatible with the DynamoDB API, so the application mostly changes its client configuration to use the DAX endpoint.",
      "ElastiCache can cache the data, but the team would have to write and maintain caching logic, which means more code changes.",
      "A replica in another Region does not bring read latency down to microseconds, and it adds cost and replication to manage.",
      "On-demand mode changes how capacity is billed and scaled. It does not lower read latency to microseconds."] },

{ id: "d2-14", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Amazon Aurora MySQL cluster that serves a reporting dashboard. Read traffic doubles during the last week of each month, and the reader instances reach 90% CPU utilization. At other times, one reader is enough.\n\nWhich solution will meet the read demand with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Configure Aurora Auto Scaling for Aurora Replicas with a target on average reader CPU utilization.",
      "Add a cross-Region read replica each month. Delete it after the reporting week ends.",
      "Move the cluster to a larger instance class before each month end, and resize it back later.",
      "Create an Amazon RDS Proxy for the cluster."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Aurora Auto Scaling adds and removes Aurora Replicas based on a target tracking policy, such as the average CPU of the readers. Applications that use the reader endpoint automatically spread reads across the replicas that it adds.",
      "Cross-Region replicas are meant for disaster recovery and local reads in another Region, and creating them by hand every month is extra work.",
      "Resizing by hand every month is manual work and can cause brief interruptions during each change.",
      "RDS Proxy pools connections. It does not add CPU capacity for read queries."] },

{ id: "d2-15", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs an application on an Amazon RDS for MySQL DB instance. Read-heavy reporting queries run throughout the day and slow down the application's writes. A CloudOps engineer must reduce the load on the primary DB instance. The reports can use slightly stale data.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create a read replica of the DB instance. Update the reporting queries to use the read replica's endpoint.",
      "Convert the DB instance to a Multi-AZ deployment. Send the reporting queries to the standby instance.",
      "Increase the automated backup retention period. Run the reporting queries against the latest snapshot.",
      "Restore a new DB instance from a snapshot each day. Run the reporting queries against the new DB instance."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A read replica is a read-only copy that Amazon RDS updates asynchronously. Routing read queries to it reduces the load on the primary, and the small replication lag is acceptable for reports.",
      "The standby in a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment cannot serve read traffic. It exists for high availability.",
      "A snapshot cannot be queried directly, and a longer retention period does not reduce the load on the primary.",
      "This can work, but it needs a daily restore process and leaves the data up to a day old, which is more work than a read replica."] },

{ id: "d2-16", d: 2,
  q: "A company has a web application that stores user session data in the memory of each Amazon EC2 instance. The Application Load Balancer uses sticky sessions. When the Auto Scaling group scales in, users on the terminated instances are logged out. Load is also uneven across the instances.\n\nWhich solution will resolve these issues?",
  o: ["Store session data in Amazon ElastiCache. Disable sticky sessions on the target group.",
      "Increase the stickiness duration on the target group.",
      "Configure scale-in protection for every instance that has active user sessions in its memory.",
      "Store session data on an Amazon EBS volume on each instance, and take snapshots of the volumes every hour."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Keeping sessions in a shared in-memory store makes the web tier stateless. Any instance can serve any request, so scale-in does not log users out, and disabling stickiness lets the load balancer spread requests evenly.",
      "Longer stickiness makes the uneven load worse, and sessions are still lost when an instance is terminated.",
      "Protecting instances that have sessions would block most scale-in and keep extra instances running.",
      "Session data on a local volume is still unavailable to the other instances, and hourly snapshots cannot restore live sessions."] },

{ id: "d2-17", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an application that uses Amazon ElastiCache with a lazy loading strategy in front of an Amazon RDS database. After prices change in the database, users sometimes see prices that are several hours out of date. The cache must continue to serve most reads.\n\nWhich solution will reduce stale data with the LEAST impact on the cache hit rate?",
  o: ["Write the new value to the cache whenever a price is updated, and set a TTL on each key.",
      "Disable the cache, and read every price directly from the RDS database instead.",
      "Flush the entire cache every 5 minutes to reload every key from the database.",
      "Increase the TTL of each price key to keep items in the cache for a longer time."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With write-through, the application updates the cache at the same time as the database, so reads see the new price immediately. A TTL acts as a safety net that expires any keys that are missed, while popular keys stay cached.",
      "Removing the cache sends every read to the database, which loses the performance benefit.",
      "Flushing everything causes a burst of cache misses and database load every 5 minutes.",
      "A longer TTL keeps stale prices in the cache for even longer."] },

{ id: "d2-18", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Amazon ElastiCache (Redis OSS) cluster with cluster mode disabled. The Evictions metric is rising steadily, and the cache hit rate is falling. The DatabaseMemoryUsagePercentage metric is near 100%, and CPU utilization is low.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Scale up to a node type with more memory, or enable cluster mode and add shards.",
      "Add more read replicas to the shard.",
      "Set the maxmemory-policy parameter to noeviction.",
      "Scale up to nodes with more vCPUs, because evictions show that the engine is CPU bound."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Evictions happen when the cache runs out of memory and removes keys to make room. More memory per node, or more shards that split the key space, increases the memory available for data, which reduces evictions and restores the hit rate.",
      "Read replicas hold copies of the same data, so they add read capacity but no extra space for more keys.",
      "With noeviction, write commands fail with errors when memory is full instead of removing keys, which breaks the application.",
      "CPU utilization is low, so the engine is not CPU bound. The evictions are caused by memory pressure."] },

{ id: "d2-19", d: 2,
  q: "A company's news website serves images and JavaScript files directly from an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to users around the world. During breaking news, the Amazon EC2 instances behind the ALB reach high CPU utilization because of requests for the same static files.\n\nWhich solution will MOST reduce the load on the instances?",
  o: ["Put an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of the site, and cache static files with long TTLs.",
      "Add more instances to the Auto Scaling group, and raise the maximum capacity of the group.",
      "Enable cross-zone load balancing to spread requests evenly across all instances.",
      "Move the ALB into a larger subnet to create more load balancer nodes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFront caches static content at edge locations close to users. Repeated requests for the same images and scripts are served from the cache instead of reaching the instances, which removes most of that load.",
      "More instances absorb the load but cost more, and every request still reaches the origin.",
      "Cross-zone load balancing is already on by default for an ALB. Spreading requests does not reduce how many requests there are.",
      "Subnet size does not limit request capacity in this way, and the instances, not the load balancer, are the bottleneck."] },

{ id: "d2-20", d: 2,
  q: "A company has a latency-sensitive API that runs on AWS Lambda functions. Users complain about slow responses for the first requests each morning because of cold starts. Traffic is high from 08:00 to 18:00 on weekdays and low at other times.\n\nWhich solution will reduce cold starts during business hours MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Use Application Auto Scaling to schedule provisioned concurrency on an alias during business hours.",
      "Configure provisioned concurrency at the peak level on the function alias 24 hours a day, every day.",
      "Set reserved concurrency on the function to the peak level.",
      "Invoke the function every minute from an Amazon EventBridge schedule to keep exactly one instance warm."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Provisioned concurrency keeps execution environments initialized, so requests avoid cold starts. Application Auto Scaling can raise it on a schedule before 08:00 and lower it after 18:00, so you pay for it only when traffic is high.",
      "This removes cold starts, but it pays for peak provisioned concurrency during nights and weekends when traffic is low.",
      "Reserved concurrency limits and guarantees how many concurrent executions a function can use. It does not pre-initialize execution environments.",
      "One warm environment does not help when many requests arrive at the same time, so most morning requests would still have cold starts."] },

{ id: "d2-21", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Amazon ECS service that runs on an Auto Scaling group of Amazon EC2 container instances. During peaks, the service needs more tasks, but new tasks stay in the PROVISIONING state because the cluster has no free capacity.\n\nWhich combination of steps will let the service scale automatically? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Configure ECS service auto scaling with a target tracking policy on CPU.",
      "Use a capacity provider with managed scaling for the Auto Scaling group.",
      "Raise the desired task count of the service to the peak value permanently.",
      "Change the task placement strategy from spread to binpack on memory only.",
      "Enable the deployment circuit breaker for the service in the cluster."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Service auto scaling uses Application Auto Scaling to change the desired number of tasks based on metrics such as average CPU, so the service adds tasks during peaks.",
      "A capacity provider with managed scaling scales the Auto Scaling group based on the capacity that tasks need. Tasks that are waiting for capacity cause the group to add instances so that the tasks can be placed.",
      "A permanently high task count wastes capacity at quiet times and still needs instances to run the tasks.",
      "Binpack can fit tasks more tightly, but it cannot place tasks when the cluster is full.",
      "The circuit breaker rolls back failed deployments. It does not add tasks or instances."] },

{ id: "d2-22", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Amazon DynamoDB table that stores sensor readings. The partition key is the sensor model. Ten thousand sensors share only three model names. The table has plenty of total provisioned capacity, but writes are throttled.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer recommend to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Use a high-cardinality partition key, such as the sensor ID.",
      "Double the table's provisioned write capacity, and keep the sensor model as the partition key.",
      "Add a local secondary index on the sensor model.",
      "Enable point-in-time recovery."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A key with only three values sends all writes to a few partitions, which become hot and throttle even when the table has spare capacity. A key with many distinct values, such as the sensor ID, spreads writes evenly. Adaptive capacity helps with uneven load but cannot fix a key with only three values.",
      "Each partition has its own throughput limit, so the hot partitions keep throttling while cost rises.",
      "Local secondary indexes share the partition key of the base table, so writes still concentrate on the same few keys.",
      "Point-in-time recovery protects against data loss. Throttled writes are rejected and never stored, so there is nothing to restore."] },

{ id: "d2-23", d: 2,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer adds an HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect to a web server configuration. All targets in an Application Load Balancer target group then become unhealthy. The health check uses HTTP on port 80 with the path /health and expects a 200 response.\n\nWhat should the CloudOps engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Exclude the /health path from the redirect.",
      "Change the health check protocol to TCP.",
      "Increase the healthy threshold count.",
      "Configure stickiness."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The redirect makes the web server answer the health check with a 301 or 302 code, which does not match the expected 200, so every target fails. Returning 200 on the health check path, or adding the redirect code to the success codes, fixes the checks.",
      "Target groups for an Application Load Balancer support only HTTP and HTTPS health checks, not TCP.",
      "A higher threshold makes targets take longer to become healthy. It does not change the redirect response.",
      "Stickiness affects how client requests are routed. It has no effect on health checks."] },

{ id: "d2-24", d: 2,
  q: "A company creates a new Application Load Balancer (ALB) for an existing fleet of Amazon EC2 instances. All targets fail health checks with the reason Request timed out. The application responds normally when a CloudOps engineer runs curl on the instances. The instances' security group allows inbound port 80 only from the company's office IP range.\n\nWhat should the CloudOps engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Allow inbound port 80 on the instance security group from the ALB's security group.",
      "Allow inbound port 80 on the ALB's security group from the instance security group.",
      "Add a route to the ALB in the route tables of the subnets that contain the instances.",
      "Change the target type of the target group from instance to IP to skip security groups."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Health checks come from the load balancer nodes, so the targets' security group must allow the health check port from the load balancer. Referencing the ALB's security group allows that traffic without hardcoding IP addresses.",
      "The ALB's inbound rules control traffic from clients to the load balancer. The timeout happens on the path from the ALB to the instances.",
      "Traffic within a VPC uses the local route, which already exists. No extra route is needed.",
      "Security groups still apply to IP targets. Changing the target type does not bypass them."] },

{ id: "d2-25", d: 2,
  q: "A company has a Network Load Balancer (NLB) with nodes in two Availability Zones. Zone A has 2 targets, and zone B has 8 targets. The 2 targets in zone A are overloaded, while the targets in zone B are mostly idle.\n\nWhich solution will spread traffic evenly across all 10 targets?",
  o: ["Enable cross-zone load balancing for the NLB.",
      "Configure sticky sessions for the NLB target group.",
      "Change the NLB target group to use IP targets.",
      "Increase the deregistration delay of the target group."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Cross-zone load balancing is off by default for Network Load Balancers, so each node sends traffic only to targets in its own zone. Each zone gets about half the traffic, so 2 targets receive as much as 8. Turning on cross-zone load balancing spreads traffic across all registered targets. Data transfer charges between zones can apply.",
      "Stickiness keeps a client on the same target. It does not balance load between zones.",
      "The target type does not change how traffic is split between zones.",
      "Deregistration delay controls how long in-flight connections can finish when a target is removed. It does not change routing."] },

{ id: "d2-26", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs an application that handles uploads that can take up to 90 seconds. Users see failed requests every time the Auto Scaling group scales in. The Application Load Balancer target group uses a deregistration delay of 10 seconds.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Increase the deregistration delay to longer than the longest request.",
      "Enable slow start mode to give new targets a gradual share of requests.",
      "Lower the health check interval to remove targets from service faster.",
      "Disable cross-zone load balancing to keep requests in each Availability Zone."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["When a target is deregistered, the load balancer stops sending it new requests but lets in-flight requests finish during the deregistration delay. A 10-second delay cuts off uploads that need up to 90 seconds, so a longer delay prevents the failures.",
      "Slow start helps new targets warm up. It does nothing for requests on targets that are being removed.",
      "Removing targets faster would cut off in-flight requests even sooner.",
      "Cross-zone settings control how traffic is spread. They do not protect requests during deregistration."] },

{ id: "d2-27", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs its primary web application behind an Application Load Balancer. Users must see a static maintenance page that is hosted in Amazon S3 whenever the application becomes unavailable. The switch must happen automatically.\n\nWhich Amazon Route 53 configuration will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Failover routing: a primary alias to the ALB with Evaluate Target Health, and a secondary record for the S3 site",
      "Weighted routing that sends 90% of traffic to the ALB and 10% to the S3 site, with no health checks on either record",
      "Latency-based routing with one record for the ALB and one record for the S3 site in the same AWS Region",
      "Multivalue answer routing that returns both the ALB and the S3 site"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Failover routing answers with the primary record while it is healthy and with the secondary record when it is not. An alias record to the ALB with Evaluate Target Health uses the load balancer's target health, so Route 53 switches to the S3 maintenance page when the application is down.",
      "Weighted routing without health checks keeps sending 90% of users to the failed ALB, and it sends 10% to the maintenance page even when the site is healthy.",
      "Latency routing picks the record with the lowest latency. Both records are in one Region, and nothing here switches traffic based on health.",
      "Multivalue answer returns several records for clients to try. It is not designed to show a maintenance page only during failures, and clients could pick it while the site is up."] },

{ id: "d2-28", d: 2,
  q: "A company uses Amazon Route 53 failover routing between two internal API endpoints that run in private subnets. The endpoints are not reachable from the internet, so Route 53 health checkers cannot connect to them. The company still needs failover based on endpoint health.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create a Route 53 health check that monitors the state of an Amazon CloudWatch alarm for each endpoint.",
      "Open each endpoint's security group to 0.0.0.0/0 to allow the Route 53 health checkers to connect.",
      "Associate the Route 53 health checks with the VPC to run the checks from inside the private subnets.",
      "Replace failover routing with simple routing that lists both endpoints as values in a single record."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Route 53 health checkers run on the internet and cannot reach private resources. A health check can instead monitor a CloudWatch alarm, such as an alarm on the endpoint's error rate. The health check becomes unhealthy when the alarm is in ALARM, which drives the failover.",
      "Opening the endpoints to the internet breaks the private design, and the subnets have no internet path anyway.",
      "Route 53 health checks cannot be placed inside a VPC. They always run from health checker locations on the internet.",
      "Simple routing with several values returns them with no health checking, so clients could still be sent to a failed endpoint."] },

{ id: "d2-29", d: 2,
  q: "A company keeps critical documents in an Amazon S3 bucket in us-east-1. For disaster recovery, a copy of every new object must be stored automatically in a bucket in eu-west-1.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Enable versioning on both buckets. Create an S3 Replication rule on the source bucket that targets the eu-west-1 bucket.",
      "Create an AWS Lambda function that copies each object. Invoke the function from an event notification on the source bucket.",
      "Create an AWS Backup plan for the source bucket. Run a weekly copy job to a backup vault in eu-west-1.",
      "Run the aws s3 sync command from an Amazon EC2 instance. Schedule the command by using a cron job on the instance."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["S3 Replication copies new objects to the destination automatically. Both buckets must have versioning enabled, and Amazon S3 needs an IAM role with replication permissions. To copy objects that already exist, use S3 Batch Replication.",
      "This can work, but the company would have to write and maintain code for retries, large objects, and failures. Replication handles these without custom code.",
      "A weekly copy job leaves up to a week of new objects without a copy in the second Region.",
      "An instance with a cron job must be patched and monitored, and new objects wait until the next run."] },

{ id: "d2-30", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an application that connects to an Amazon RDS for MySQL Multi-AZ DB instance by using the DB endpoint. During a test failover, Amazon RDS promotes the standby within 60 seconds, but the application continues to fail for 20 minutes until it is restarted.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The application caches the DNS lookup of the endpoint for longer than the record's TTL.",
      "The standby instance is in a different DB subnet group that the application cannot reach.",
      "The standby needs 20 minutes to replay transaction logs before it accepts connections.",
      "Multi-AZ failover creates a new endpoint name that the application must be updated to use."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["During a Multi-AZ failover, RDS updates the endpoint's DNS record to point to the new primary. The endpoint name stays the same, but an application or runtime that caches DNS results for too long keeps connecting to the old address. Honoring the short TTL, or connecting through RDS Proxy, shortens recovery.",
      "The primary and standby are in the same DB subnet group and VPC, so the application's network path is the same.",
      "The standby is kept current with synchronous replication, and the scenario says the promotion finished within 60 seconds.",
      "The endpoint name does not change during a Multi-AZ failover. Only the IP address behind it changes."] },

{ id: "d2-31", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL in a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment. The company needs faster failover, typically under 35 seconds, and needs to use the standby capacity for read queries.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Move to a Multi-AZ DB cluster deployment with two readable standby instances.",
      "Add a read replica in another Region. Promote it during an outage.",
      "Enable RDS storage autoscaling.",
      "Keep the Multi-AZ DB instance deployment. Send read queries to its standby instance."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A Multi-AZ DB cluster has a writer and two readable standby instances in three Availability Zones. Failover is typically under 35 seconds, and applications can send read queries to the reader endpoint.",
      "Promoting a cross-Region replica is a manual disaster recovery step that takes longer and changes the endpoint.",
      "Storage autoscaling grows storage when free space runs low. It has no effect on failover time.",
      "In a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment, the standby cannot serve reads. It exists only for failover."] },

{ id: "d2-32", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Amazon Aurora MySQL cluster with a writer and three Aurora Replicas: two db.r6g.2xlarge replicas that the application uses, and one db.r6g.large replica for ad hoc reports. During a failover test, Aurora promoted the small reporting replica, and the application performed poorly.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer ensure that a large replica is promoted during failover?",
  o: ["Set the large replicas to promotion tier 0 and the reporting replica to tier 15.",
      "Move the reporting replica to a different Availability Zone from the one the writer uses.",
      "Add the large replicas to a custom endpoint named failover for the cluster.",
      "Enable Aurora Backtrack."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Aurora promotes the replica with the highest priority, which is the lowest tier number. Tier 0 for the large replicas and tier 15 for the reporting replica makes Aurora choose a large replica. Among replicas in the same tier, Aurora picks the largest one.",
      "Availability Zone placement does not decide which replica is promoted.",
      "Custom endpoints group instances for connections. They do not affect which replica is promoted.",
      "Backtrack rewinds a cluster's data to an earlier point in time. It does not control failover."] },

{ id: "d2-33", d: 2,
  q: "A company has a financial application that uses Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL in us-east-1. The company needs a disaster recovery plan for a Regional outage with an RPO of about 1 second and an RTO of a few minutes. The company also needs low-latency reads in eu-west-1.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Convert the cluster to an Aurora Global Database with a secondary cluster in eu-west-1.",
      "Take hourly cluster snapshots, and copy each snapshot to eu-west-1 by using an AWS Backup plan.",
      "Create a Multi-AZ DB cluster in us-east-1 that spans three Availability Zones.",
      "Export the database to Amazon S3 each night, and import it into a cluster in eu-west-1."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Aurora Global Database replicates storage to secondary Regions with typical lag of under 1 second, and the secondary cluster serves reads locally. During a Regional outage, a secondary cluster can be promoted to accept writes within minutes.",
      "Hourly snapshots give an RPO of up to an hour, and restoring a cluster from a snapshot takes much longer than a few minutes.",
      "Multi-AZ protects against an Availability Zone failure, not a Regional outage, and it provides no reads in eu-west-1.",
      "Nightly exports can lose up to a day of data, which fails the 1-second RPO."] },

{ id: "d2-34", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an application on Amazon EC2 instances in three Availability Zones. The instances share files through an Amazon EFS file system that uses the One Zone storage class. When the file system's Availability Zone had an outage, all instances lost access to the shared files.\n\nWhich solution will prevent this issue in the future?",
  o: ["Move the data to an EFS file system with Regional storage and a mount target in each zone.",
      "Add mount targets for the One Zone file system in the two other Availability Zones.",
      "Configure EFS Replication to another One Zone file system in the same Availability Zone.",
      "Enable Elastic throughput for the One Zone file system."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Regional EFS file systems store data redundantly across multiple Availability Zones, and a mount target in each zone gives instances local access. The file system stays available if one zone fails.",
      "A One Zone file system stores data in one zone and has a mount target only in that zone. Instances in other zones still lose access when that zone fails.",
      "A replica in the same zone fails along with the source, so it does not protect against a zone outage.",
      "The throughput mode affects performance, not where the data is stored."] },

{ id: "d2-35", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs an application behind an Application Load Balancer in three Availability Zones. During an incident, monitoring shows elevated errors in one Availability Zone, but health checks still pass. The company must move traffic away from that Availability Zone quickly and move it back later, without changing any infrastructure.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Start a zonal shift in Amazon Application Recovery Controller to move the ALB's traffic away from the zone.",
      "Deregister the targets in the impaired zone from the target group, and register them again after the event.",
      "Update the Auto Scaling group to remove the impaired zone's subnet. Add the subnet back after the event.",
      "Lower the health check thresholds."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A zonal shift moves traffic for a supported resource, such as a load balancer, away from one Availability Zone for a duration that you set. It works even when health checks pass, changes no infrastructure, and can be canceled or allowed to expire to restore traffic. The load balancer must be opted in to zonal shift beforehand, and the other zones need enough capacity to absorb the load.",
      "Deregistering and registering targets by hand is slow and error prone during an incident, and it must be undone carefully.",
      "Changing the group's subnets terminates and relaunches instances, which is a slow infrastructure change.",
      "Lower thresholds affect every zone and may not catch the problem if checks still succeed. They also stay in place after the event."] },

{ id: "d2-36", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs a critical application in an Auto Scaling group across two Availability Zones with 4 instances in each Availability Zone. The application needs at least 8 instances to serve peak load. The application must continue to serve peak load if one Availability Zone fails, without waiting for new instances to launch.\n\nWhich configuration will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Run 4 instances in each of three Availability Zones.",
      "Run 6 instances in each of two Availability Zones.",
      "Run 8 instances in a single Availability Zone only.",
      "Run 4 instances in each of two Availability Zones. Set the maximum capacity to 16."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With 12 instances across three zones, losing any one zone leaves 8 instances, which is enough for peak load. This is static stability: enough capacity is already running, so recovery does not depend on launching instances during the event.",
      "Losing one zone leaves only 6 instances, which is below the 8 needed, so the group would have to launch more during the event.",
      "A single zone is a single point of failure. Losing that zone takes down the whole application.",
      "A higher maximum lets the group scale out, but it must launch instances after the zone fails, which takes time and might be constrained during a zone event."] },

{ id: "d2-37", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Amazon ElastiCache (Redis OSS) replication group with one primary node and no replicas. When the primary node failed, the application lost its cache for 30 minutes while a new node was created and filled with data.\n\nWhich solution will provide the FASTEST recovery from a primary node failure?",
  o: ["Add a replica in another Availability Zone. Enable Multi-AZ with automatic failover.",
      "Configure daily automatic backups. Restore the latest backup whenever the primary node fails.",
      "Change the node type to a larger size to start a new primary node and load data faster.",
      "Enable data tiering to store the cache data on SSDs that survive the node failure."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With Multi-AZ and automatic failover, ElastiCache promotes a replica in another Availability Zone when the primary fails, and the replica already holds the data. The primary endpoint stays the same, so the application reconnects without configuration changes.",
      "Restoring from a backup creates a new cluster, still takes a long time, and loses data written since the backup.",
      "A larger node still has to be created and refilled with data, so recovery is not much faster.",
      "Data tiering extends capacity by using SSDs on the same node. It does not protect against that node failing."] },

{ id: "d2-38", d: 2,
  q: "A company must back up every Amazon EBS volume, Amazon RDS database, and Amazon DynamoDB table that has the tag Backup=Daily. Backups must be kept for 35 days, and a copy must be stored in a second AWS Region. New resources that get the tag must be included automatically.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create an AWS Backup plan with a daily rule, 35-day retention, and a copy action to a vault in the second Region.",
      "Assign resources to the backup plan by using the Backup=Daily tag.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge schedule that invokes an AWS Lambda function to snapshot each tagged resource every day.",
      "Use Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager for all three resource types, with a cross-Region copy rule.",
      "Configure Amazon S3 Cross-Region Replication for the bucket where AWS Backup stores the recovery points."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["An AWS Backup plan defines when backups run and how long they are kept. A copy action sends each recovery point to a backup vault in another Region, with its own retention.",
      "Tag-based resource assignment includes every supported resource that has the tag, including resources created later, so no one has to update the plan.",
      "Custom snapshot code must be built and maintained for each resource type, and it still needs separate retention and copy logic.",
      "Data Lifecycle Manager manages EBS snapshots and EBS-backed AMIs. It does not back up RDS databases or DynamoDB tables.",
      "AWS Backup stores recovery points in backup vaults, not in an S3 bucket that you manage, so there is no bucket to replicate."] },

{ id: "d2-39", d: 2,
  q: "A company has production AWS accounts in an organization in AWS Organizations. The company's security team needs backups of the production accounts stored in a separate backup account, so that a compromised production account cannot delete them.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Enable cross-account backup in AWS Backup. Add a copy action to a vault in the backup account.",
      "Share each production backup vault with the backup account by using AWS Resource Access Manager.",
      "Give the backup account's IAM users permission to start backup jobs inside each production account.",
      "Create a second backup vault in each production account. Copy every recovery point into it."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With cross-account backup enabled in the organization's management account, a backup plan can copy recovery points to a vault in another account. Production administrators cannot delete copies that live in the backup account, and a vault access policy can restrict access further.",
      "Standard backup vaults are not shared through AWS RAM, and the backups would still be stored in the production accounts.",
      "Backups created inside the production accounts still live there and can be deleted from a compromised account.",
      "A second vault in the same account is still controlled by that account, so a compromise could delete both copies."] },

{ id: "d2-40", d: 2,
  q: "A company must keep backups for 7 years to comply with a regulation. No one, including the root user, can be allowed to delete the backups before the retention period ends.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Apply AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode to the vault, with a minimum retention of 7 years.",
      "Apply AWS Backup Vault Lock in governance mode to the vault, with a minimum retention of 7 years.",
      "Add a vault access policy that denies backup:DeleteRecoveryPoint to all IAM users in the account.",
      "Enable MFA delete for the backup vault."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Vault Lock in compliance mode makes the retention settings immutable after the grace period ends. No one, including the root user and AWS, can delete recovery points early or remove the lock.",
      "Governance mode can be removed or changed by users who have sufficient IAM permissions, so it does not stop all deletions.",
      "Users with enough permissions, or the root user, can change the vault access policy, so it is not an immutable control.",
      "MFA delete is an S3 bucket feature. Backup vaults use Vault Lock for this purpose."] },

{ id: "d2-41", d: 2,
  q: "A company stores shared files on an Amazon EFS file system in us-east-1. For disaster recovery, the company needs a copy in us-west-2 that is no more than about 15 minutes behind the source.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST operationally efficient way?",
  o: ["Enable EFS Replication with a destination file system in us-west-2.",
      "Run an AWS DataSync task every hour from us-east-1 to an EFS file system in us-west-2.",
      "Mount both file systems on an Amazon EC2 instance, and run rsync from a cron job every 15 minutes.",
      "Back up the file system with AWS Backup every day, and copy each backup to us-west-2."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["EFS Replication keeps a read-only replica in another Region, with a recovery point objective of 15 minutes for most file systems, and it needs no scripts or schedules. During a disaster, you fail over to the replica.",
      "DataSync is built for scheduled transfers, so it adds tasks to run, and an hourly schedule does not meet the 15-minute target.",
      "A cron job on a server must be patched, monitored, and retried, which is far more overhead than a managed replication feature.",
      "A daily backup can lose up to a day of changes, which fails the 15-minute target."] },

{ id: "d2-42", d: 2,
  q: "A company's auditors require monthly evidence that backups of critical Amazon RDS databases and Amazon EBS volumes can be restored, and how long each restore takes. The company's CloudOps team currently performs these restores manually.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an AWS Backup restore testing plan that restores recent recovery points on a schedule.",
      "Write an AWS Lambda function that restores each backup monthly, times it, and saves results to a spreadsheet.",
      "Configure AWS Backup Audit Manager with a framework that checks that a backup plan covers each resource.",
      "Copy every recovery point to a second Region each month."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Restore testing runs scheduled restores of selected recovery points, records whether each restore succeeded and how long it took, and then cleans up the test resources. This gives auditors monthly evidence with no manual restores.",
      "A custom function works, but it is code to build and maintain for each resource type.",
      "Audit Manager reports whether backup controls, such as plans and retention, are in place. On its own, it does not run restores to prove that they work.",
      "Copies prove that data was duplicated, not that it can be restored."] },

{ id: "d2-43", d: 2,
  q: "A company needs hourly snapshots of Amazon EBS volumes that have the tag Tier=Critical, kept for 48 hours. The company also needs daily snapshots of the same volumes, kept for 30 days. The company wants a policy-based feature that is built into Amazon EBS and needs no custom code.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager policy that targets the tag and has two schedules.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that runs every hour and invokes an AWS Lambda function to snapshot the volumes.",
      "Enable fast snapshot restore for the tagged volumes.",
      "Create an Auto Scaling scheduled action that creates snapshots of the volumes every hour."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Data Lifecycle Manager automates the creation, retention, and deletion of EBS snapshots for volumes or instances selected by tag. One policy can have several schedules, such as hourly snapshots kept for 48 hours and daily snapshots kept for 30 days.",
      "Custom code must handle creating, keeping, and deleting snapshots, which Data Lifecycle Manager provides natively.",
      "Fast snapshot restore speeds up creating volumes from existing snapshots. It does not create snapshots.",
      "Auto Scaling scheduled actions change a group's capacity. They cannot create EBS snapshots."] },

{ id: "d2-44", d: 2,
  q: "A company stores application logs in Amazon S3 Standard. The logs are read often for 30 days and rarely after that. The logs must be kept for 1 year for compliance and must be retrievable within hours. After 1 year, the logs must be deleted.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Create an S3 Lifecycle configuration that transitions objects to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 30 days and expires objects after 365 days.",
      "Create an S3 Lifecycle configuration that transitions objects to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days and expires objects after 365 days.",
      "Create an AWS Lambda function that runs each day to copy objects older than 30 days to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval and delete objects older than 1 year.",
      "Enable S3 Object Lock in compliance mode with a retention period of 1 year. Keep all objects in S3 Standard for the full retention period."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["S3 Lifecycle moves objects to a lower-cost storage class and deletes them at the end of the retention period without custom code. S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval costs less than S3 Standard-IA and supports retrieval within hours, which meets the requirement.",
      "This meets the requirements, but S3 Standard-IA costs more than S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval for logs that are rarely read and can wait hours for retrieval.",
      "This re-creates what a lifecycle rule does for free, and the company would have to maintain the code.",
      "Object Lock prevents deletion, which the requirement does not ask for, and keeping everything in S3 Standard is the most expensive choice."] },

{ id: "d2-45", d: 2,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer accidentally deleted several Amazon EBS snapshots and AMIs that were needed for recovery. The company must be able to recover deleted snapshots and AMIs for 14 days after they are deleted.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create Recycle Bin retention rules for EBS snapshots and for AMIs with a 14-day retention period.",
      "Enable AWS Config recording to restore deleted snapshots and AMIs from configuration history.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail data events for Amazon EBS to re-create each deleted snapshot from the trail.",
      "Add an IAM policy that denies ec2:DeleteSnapshot to all users, including the backup automation role."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Recycle Bin keeps deleted EBS snapshots and EBS-backed AMIs for the retention period of a matching rule. During that time you can recover them, and after that they are permanently deleted.",
      "Config records configuration history. It cannot restore the data in a deleted snapshot.",
      "CloudTrail records the delete API call. The snapshot data is gone and cannot be rebuilt from a log.",
      "Denying all deletions breaks the backup automation's retention cleanup, and snapshot costs would grow without limit."] },

{ id: "d2-46", d: 2,
  q: "A company has a production Amazon RDS for MySQL database. At 14:05, a developer accidentally ran a DELETE statement without a WHERE clause. Automated backups are enabled with a 7-day retention period. The company needs the data as it was at 14:04.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Restore the DB instance to a point in time of 14:04 as a new DB instance. Point the application to it.",
      "Restore the most recent daily automated snapshot onto the existing DB instance to overwrite its current data.",
      "Promote the Multi-AZ standby instance, because the standby still has the data from before 14:05.",
      "Create a read replica of the DB instance. Promote the replica to recover the data from before 14:05."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Point-in-time restore uses automated backups and transaction logs to create a new DB instance at any second within the retention period, up to about 5 minutes before the current time. A restore always creates a new instance, so the application must be pointed to the new endpoint, or the instances must be renamed.",
      "RDS restores always create a new DB instance. You cannot restore a snapshot onto an existing instance, and a daily snapshot would lose more data.",
      "The standby is synchronously replicated, so the DELETE was applied to it as well.",
      "A new replica copies the current data, which already has the rows deleted."] },

{ id: "d2-47", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs a stateless web application on a single Amazon EC2 instance in one Availability Zone. An outage in the Availability Zone took the application offline. The application must remain available if an Availability Zone fails and must replace failed instances automatically.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an Auto Scaling group that spans multiple Availability Zones. Place an Application Load Balancer in front of the group.",
      "Create an Auto Scaling group in the existing Availability Zone. Set the minimum capacity of the group to 4 instances.",
      "Change the instance to a larger instance type. Create an Amazon CloudWatch alarm that uses the EC2 recover action.",
      "Launch a second instance in another Availability Zone. Create an Amazon Route 53 simple record with both IP addresses."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An Auto Scaling group across Availability Zones replaces failed instances and rebalances capacity. An Application Load Balancer sends traffic only to healthy targets in the zones that are still working. This is the standard pattern for a highly available stateless application.",
      "All instances are still in one Availability Zone, so an outage in that zone stops the application.",
      "The recover action helps with hardware problems on one instance. It does not protect against the loss of the whole Availability Zone.",
      "A simple record has no health checks, so clients still receive the failed instance's IP address, and nothing replaces failed instances."] },

{ id: "d2-48", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs a production database on a Single-AZ Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance. The database must fail over automatically if the Availability Zone fails. Committed transactions must not be lost, and the application must continue to use the same endpoint.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Modify the DB instance to use a Multi-AZ deployment with a synchronous standby in another Availability Zone.",
      "Create a read replica in another Availability Zone. Promote the read replica during an Availability Zone outage.",
      "Take a manual snapshot every hour. Restore the latest snapshot in another Availability Zone during an outage.",
      "Install PostgreSQL on an Amazon EC2 instance in another Availability Zone. Configure log shipping to the instance."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A Multi-AZ DB instance deployment keeps a synchronous standby in another Availability Zone. Amazon RDS fails over automatically and updates the DNS record of the DB endpoint, so the application reconnects to the same endpoint.",
      "Replication to a read replica is asynchronous, so committed transactions can be lost. Promotion is manual, and the replica has a different endpoint.",
      "A restore loses the changes made since the last snapshot, and the restored DB instance has a new endpoint.",
      "The company would have to patch and fail over this database itself, and log shipping does not fail over automatically."] },

{ id: "d2-49", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Amazon DynamoDB table with point-in-time recovery enabled. A bug in a deployment 2 hours ago overwrote attributes in thousands of items. The company needs the data as it was before the bug.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Restore the table to a point in time before the deployment as a new table. Copy the needed data back.",
      "Restore the table in place to a point in time before the deployment.",
      "Enable DynamoDB Streams, and replay the stream records from the last 2 hours in reverse order.",
      "Use the on-demand backup that DynamoDB created automatically when point-in-time recovery was enabled."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Point-in-time recovery restores a table to any second within its recovery period, which can be set from 1 to 35 days. The restore always creates a new table, so the team can copy the correct items back or switch the application to the restored table.",
      "DynamoDB cannot restore a table in place. Every restore creates a new table.",
      "Turning on Streams now captures only future changes, so the earlier values are not available this way.",
      "Turning on point-in-time recovery does not create an on-demand backup. The two are separate features."] },

{ id: "d2-50", d: 2,
  q: "A global ecommerce company needs its Amazon DynamoDB shopping cart table to accept writes in us-east-1 and eu-west-1 at the same time. Changes must replicate between the AWS Regions automatically, and the application must continue to work if one Region fails.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Convert the table to a DynamoDB global table with replicas in both Regions.",
      "Enable DynamoDB Streams. Use an AWS Lambda function to copy writes to a table in the other Region.",
      "Take on-demand backups every hour, and restore them in the other Region.",
      "Use AWS Backup to copy the table to the other Region every 15 minutes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Global tables provide multi-Region, multi-active replication. Applications can read and write in each replica Region, DynamoDB replicates changes automatically, and the application can keep working in one Region if the other fails.",
      "Custom stream replication is code to build and operate, and handling conflicts and failures yourself is complex.",
      "Hourly backups lose up to an hour of carts, and restored tables are separate copies that cannot both accept writes and stay in sync.",
      "Periodic copies are not live replicas, so the second Region cannot accept writes that stay in sync."] },

{ id: "d2-51", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Amazon S3 bucket with versioning enabled. A user accidentally deleted an important report with a simple DELETE request that did not specify a version ID. The report no longer appears in the bucket listing.\n\nHow can a CloudOps engineer restore the report with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Delete the delete marker that is now the current version of the object.",
      "Rebuild the object from the S3 server access logs that recorded the upload.",
      "Create a new bucket with versioning enabled. Copy the object back from it.",
      "Ask AWS Support to recover the object from the bucket's deleted storage."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["In a versioned bucket, a simple DELETE adds a delete marker as the current version and keeps the earlier versions. Removing the delete marker makes the previous version current again, so the report reappears.",
      "Server access logs record requests, not object contents, so they cannot rebuild the report.",
      "A new bucket does not contain the deleted object, so there is nothing to copy back.",
      "No support request is needed, because the earlier version is still stored in the bucket."] },

{ id: "d2-52", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Amazon S3 bucket with versioning enabled. The bucket stores files that are overwritten many times each day. Storage costs continue to rise because every old version is kept indefinitely. The company must be able to recover overwritten files for 30 days.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add a lifecycle rule that permanently deletes noncurrent versions 30 days after they become noncurrent.",
      "Suspend versioning on the bucket to stop keeping old versions of the overwritten files.",
      "Add a lifecycle rule that expires the current versions of all objects 30 days after creation.",
      "Enable S3 Intelligent-Tiering to move the old versions to a cheaper tier after 30 days."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The NoncurrentVersionExpiration action deletes old versions a set number of days after they are replaced. Overwritten files stay recoverable for 30 days, and storage no longer grows without limit.",
      "Suspending versioning stops keeping new versions, so overwritten files could no longer be recovered, and existing old versions would remain.",
      "Expiring current versions deletes live files, which the company does not want, and it does not remove old versions.",
      "Intelligent-Tiering lowers the price of data that is not accessed, but it keeps every version forever, so costs still grow."] },

{ id: "d2-53", d: 2,
  q: "A company stores trading records in Amazon S3. Regulations require that no user can overwrite or delete a record for 5 years. Separately, the company's legal team sometimes needs to keep specific records indefinitely during an investigation.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Enable S3 Object Lock. Set a default retention of 5 years in compliance mode.",
      "Place a legal hold on specific object versions during an investigation.",
      "Set a default retention of 5 years in governance mode with no extra permissions.",
      "Add a bucket policy that denies s3:DeleteObject to every user except the root user.",
      "Enable MFA delete."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Object Lock in compliance mode prevents every user, including the root user, from overwriting or deleting a protected version until its retention period ends, and the retention period cannot be shortened.",
      "A legal hold prevents an object version from being deleted or overwritten until the hold is removed. It has no expiration date, which suits investigations of unknown length.",
      "Users with the s3:BypassGovernanceRetention permission can delete objects in governance mode, so it does not meet a rule that applies to every user.",
      "Administrators can change a bucket policy, and the root user would still be able to delete records.",
      "MFA delete makes deletions harder, but the root user can still delete versions, and it does not set a retention period."] },

{ id: "d2-54", d: 2,
  q: "A company enables Amazon S3 Cross-Region Replication from a bucket in us-east-1 to a bucket in us-west-2 for disaster recovery. New objects replicate correctly, but the 2 million objects that existed before replication was configured are missing from the destination. The company also needs 99.9% of new objects replicated within 15 minutes.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Run an S3 Batch Replication job to replicate the existing objects.",
      "Enable S3 Replication Time Control for the replication rule.",
      "Configure S3 Transfer Acceleration for the source bucket.",
      "Suspend and then resume versioning on the source bucket.",
      "Add a lifecycle rule that copies existing objects to us-west-2."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Replication rules apply only to objects written after the rule is created. S3 Batch Replication replicates existing objects, and objects that failed to replicate before, on demand.",
      "Replication Time Control replicates most new objects in seconds and 99.9% of them within 15 minutes, backed by a service level agreement, and it adds replication metrics and notifications.",
      "Transfer Acceleration speeds up client transfers to a bucket. It does not affect replication.",
      "Changing the versioning state does not replicate existing objects, and replication requires versioning to stay enabled.",
      "Lifecycle rules transition or expire objects. They cannot copy objects to another Region."] },

{ id: "d2-55", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Amazon FSx for Windows File Server share. Users often overwrite documents by mistake and open tickets to get earlier versions back. Users must be able to restore earlier versions of their files themselves from Windows File Explorer.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer configure to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Enable shadow copies for the FSx file system on a regular schedule.",
      "Configure daily automatic backups of the FSx file system with 30-day retention.",
      "Enable Amazon S3 Versioning for the bucket that stores the FSx file system data.",
      "Configure data deduplication for the FSx file system to keep copies of changes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Shadow copies are point-in-time snapshots of the file system that Windows users can browse on the Previous Versions tab in File Explorer. Users can restore earlier versions of their own files without opening a ticket.",
      "Automatic backups protect the whole file system, but restoring one creates a new file system. That needs an administrator and does not give users self-service restores.",
      "FSx for Windows File Server data is not stored in a bucket that you manage, so there is no S3 Versioning to enable.",
      "Deduplication saves storage by removing duplicate data. It does not keep earlier versions of files."] },

{ id: "d2-56", d: 2,
  q: "A company copies the AMIs of its application servers from us-east-1 to us-west-2 for disaster recovery. The AMIs' Amazon EBS snapshots are encrypted with a customer managed AWS KMS key that is in us-east-1. The copies must be encrypted with a customer managed key.\n\nWhat must a CloudOps engineer specify when copying the AMIs?",
  o: ["A KMS key in us-west-2 to encrypt the copied snapshots, because KMS keys are Regional.",
      "The same KMS key ARN from us-east-1, because AMI copies keep the source Region's key.",
      "No key at all, because AMI copies across Regions are always left unencrypted by default.",
      "The AWS owned key for EBS, because customer managed keys cannot be used across Regions."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["KMS keys are Regional, so a key in us-east-1 cannot encrypt data in us-west-2. When you copy an AMI with encrypted snapshots to another Region, you specify a key in the destination Region, such as a customer managed key or a replica of a multi-Region key.",
      "A single-Region key exists only in its own Region and cannot encrypt the copies in us-west-2.",
      "Copies of encrypted snapshots are always encrypted. They cannot become unencrypted.",
      "EBS uses the AWS managed key aws/ebs by default, not an AWS owned key. Customer managed keys work as long as the key is in the destination Region."] },

{ id: "d2-57", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an internal wiki. During a Regional disaster, the wiki can be down for up to 24 hours, and losing up to 12 hours of data is acceptable. The company needs the lowest-cost disaster recovery strategy.\n\nWhich strategy should a CloudOps engineer implement?",
  o: ["Backup and restore, with backups copied to a second Region",
      "Pilot light, with a live database replica in a second Region",
      "Warm standby, with a scaled-down copy running in a second Region",
      "Multi-site active/active across two Regions"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Backup and restore costs the least because nothing runs in the recovery Region until a disaster. Copying backups to a second Region every few hours meets a 12-hour RPO, and rebuilding from infrastructure as code and backups fits a 24-hour RTO.",
      "Pilot light keeps core components such as a database replica running all the time, which costs more than these targets need.",
      "Warm standby runs a scaled-down copy of the whole workload, which costs more than a 24-hour RTO requires.",
      "Active/active gives near-zero RTO and RPO at the highest cost, far beyond what the wiki needs."] },

{ id: "d2-58", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an order system that must recover from a Regional disaster within 4 hours with no more than 5 minutes of data loss. To keep costs low, the company does not want application servers running in the recovery AWS Region.\n\nWhich disaster recovery strategy will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Pilot light: replicate the database continuously, and keep application servers off until failover",
      "Backup and restore: copy nightly database snapshots to the recovery Region, and rebuild there",
      "Warm standby: run a scaled-down copy of every tier in the recovery Region",
      "Multi-site active/active: serve traffic from both Regions at all times"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Pilot light keeps the core data, such as a cross-Region database replica, continuously up to date, which meets a 5-minute RPO. Application servers are created from AMIs and infrastructure as code only during a failover, which fits a 4-hour RTO at low cost.",
      "Nightly snapshots could lose up to a day of orders, which fails the 5-minute RPO.",
      "Warm standby meets the targets but keeps application servers running all the time, which the company wants to avoid.",
      "Active/active has the highest cost and runs full capacity in both Regions."] },

{ id: "d2-59", d: 2,
  q: "A company has a customer-facing application that must recover from a Regional disaster in under 15 minutes with an RPO of seconds. The company is willing to pay for a smaller, always-running copy of the workload in the recovery AWS Region, but not for full duplicate capacity.\n\nWhich disaster recovery strategy will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Warm standby",
      "Pilot light",
      "Backup and restore",
      "Multi-site active/active"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Warm standby runs a scaled-down but fully working copy of the workload in the recovery Region, with data replicated continuously. During a disaster, the copy scales up and takes traffic within minutes.",
      "Pilot light keeps only core components running, so application servers must be started and scaled during failover, which usually takes longer than 15 minutes.",
      "Backup and restore rebuilds everything from backups, which takes hours and loses the data written since the last backup.",
      "Active/active meets the targets but runs full capacity in both Regions, which the company does not want to pay for."] },

{ id: "d2-60", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance in us-east-1 and keeps a cross-Region read replica in us-west-2 for disaster recovery. During a Regional outage drill, the database in us-west-2 must start accepting writes.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Promote the read replica in us-west-2 to a standalone DB instance. Point the application to it.",
      "Start a Multi-AZ failover on the primary instance.",
      "Change the replica's parameter group to set read_only to 0, and then reboot the replica instance.",
      "Restore the latest snapshot of the primary instance in us-west-2. Delete the read replica afterward."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Promoting a read replica stops replication and turns the replica into a standalone DB instance that accepts writes. Applications then connect to the promoted instance's endpoint, often through a DNS record that is updated during the failover.",
      "Multi-AZ failover switches to a standby in the same Region. It does not involve cross-Region read replicas, and it cannot run if the primary Region is down.",
      "RDS manages replication on read replicas. Changing a parameter does not turn a replica into a writable primary.",
      "Restoring a snapshot loses data written since the snapshot and takes longer than promoting the replica, which already has current data."] },

{ id: "d2-61", d: 2,
  q: "A company has an Amazon EC2 instance in us-east-1a with an Amazon EBS data volume that has hourly snapshots. The us-east-1a Availability Zone is impaired, and the company needs the data online on a new instance in us-east-1b.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create a new volume from the latest snapshot in us-east-1b. Attach it to the new instance.",
      "Detach the volume in us-east-1a, and attach it directly to the new instance in us-east-1b.",
      "Copy the snapshot to us-east-1b first, because each EBS snapshot is stored only in the zone of its volume.",
      "Configure EBS Multi-Attach."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["EBS snapshots are stored regionally, so a volume can be created from a snapshot in any Availability Zone in the Region. The new volume in us-east-1b holds the data as of the last snapshot.",
      "An EBS volume can be attached only to instances in its own Availability Zone.",
      "Snapshots are already available to every zone in the Region. Snapshot copies are for moving snapshots between Regions or accounts, or for changing encryption.",
      "Multi-Attach works only for io1 and io2 volumes and only with instances in the same Availability Zone."] },

{ id: "d2-62", d: 2,
  q: "A company keeps automated backups for an Amazon RDS database for 35 days, the maximum. A new policy requires monthly backups to be kept for 1 year and then deleted automatically.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an AWS Backup plan with a monthly rule for the database and a 1-year retention period.",
      "Change the automated backup retention period of the DB instance from 35 days to 365 days.",
      "Ask a CloudOps engineer to take a manual snapshot each month and delete snapshots older than 1 year.",
      "Enable Multi-AZ for the DB instance."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Backup can take monthly snapshots of RDS databases, keep them for the retention period that you set, and delete them automatically. These snapshots are not limited by the 35-day window for automated backups.",
      "The maximum retention for RDS automated backups is 35 days, so 365 days is not allowed.",
      "Manual snapshots meet the retention need, but taking and deleting them by hand is effort and easy to forget.",
      "A Multi-AZ standby is a live copy for failover. It does not keep historical backups."] },

{ id: "d2-63", d: 2,
  q: "A company backs up Amazon EC2 instances that run Microsoft SQL Server on Windows. Restores from crash-consistent backups sometimes require database recovery steps. The company needs application-consistent backups without stopping the instances.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Enable Windows VSS in the AWS Backup plan for the EC2 instances.",
      "Stop the instances before each backup by using a scheduled AWS Lambda function.",
      "Take Amazon EBS snapshots of each volume at different times to spread the I/O load.",
      "Configure fast snapshot restore for the snapshots that AWS Backup creates."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With the Windows VSS option enabled, AWS Backup uses the Volume Shadow Copy Service through SSM Agent to quiesce applications such as SQL Server before the snapshot. This creates application-consistent backups without stopping the instance. The instance needs SSM Agent and the required IAM permissions.",
      "Stopping instances gives consistent backups but causes downtime, which the company wants to avoid.",
      "Snapshots taken at different times are not consistent with each other, which makes the problem worse.",
      "Fast snapshot restore speeds up volume creation. It does not make backups application consistent."] },

{ id: "d2-64", d: 2,
  q: "A company completes a business impact analysis for its billing system. The system can lose at most 15 minutes of data and must be back online within 2 hours after a disaster.\n\nWhich statement correctly describes these targets?",
  o: ["The RPO is 15 minutes, and the RTO is 2 hours.",
      "The RTO is 15 minutes, and the RPO is 2 hours.",
      "The RPO and the RTO are both 2 hours and 15 minutes.",
      "The RPO is 2 hours, and the SLA is 15 minutes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The recovery point objective (RPO) is the maximum acceptable data loss, measured as the time since the last recovery point, which is 15 minutes here. The recovery time objective (RTO) is the maximum acceptable time to restore service, which is 2 hours.",
      "This reverses the definitions. RTO is about downtime, and RPO is about data loss.",
      "RPO and RTO are separate targets. They are not added together.",
      "The 2-hour target is the RTO, and an SLA is a service commitment, not a data loss target."] },

{ id: "d2-65", d: 2,
  q: "A company must be able to restore the objects in an Amazon S3 bucket to their state at any point in the past 14 days after accidental bulk changes. Versioning is already enabled for the bucket.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Use AWS Backup continuous backups for the bucket with 14-day retention. Restore to a point in time.",
      "Write a script that reads all object versions and copies the versions from the chosen time back into place.",
      "Enable S3 Cross-Region Replication.",
      "Add a lifecycle rule that moves noncurrent versions to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 14 days."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Backup supports continuous backups for Amazon S3, which allow restores to any point in time within a retention period of up to 35 days. The bucket must have versioning enabled, which it already does.",
      "This can work, but writing and testing a script that rebuilds a bucket from its versions is a lot of effort and error prone.",
      "Replication copies changes as they happen, including the accidental changes, so the replica would not keep the older state.",
      "Moving old versions to Deep Archive reduces cost but makes them slow to restore, and it does not provide a point-in-time restore."] },
]);
